r 1st access \u2192 truthy \u2192 not truthy = False. Condition 1 = False. \u2713\n- Condition 2: `self.prerelease` \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 False. Condition 2 = False. \u2713 (other not accessed)\n- Condition 3: `not self.prerelease` \u2192 True. `other.prerelease` \u2192 2nd access \u2192 falsy \u2192 False. Condition 3 = False. \u2713\n- Condition 4: `self.prerelease` \u2192 falsy \u2192 False. Condition 4 = False. \u2713\n- \u2192 else \u2192 line 203 executed! AssertionError raised.\n\nSo the test would be:\n\n```python\ndef test_cmp_unreachable_else():\n    class Flaky(StrictVersion):\n        def __init__(self, vstring):\n            super().__init__(vstring)\n            self._accesses = 0\n        @property\n        def prerelease(self):\n            self._accesses += 1\n            if self._accesses == 1:\n                return ('a', 1)  # truthy\n            return None  # falsy\n    self_v = Flaky('1.0')\n    other_v = Flaky('1.0')\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError, match=\"never get here\"):\n        self_v._cmp(other_v)\n```\n\nWait, but `Flaky.__init__` calls `super().__init__(vstring)` which calls `self.parse(vstring)` which sets `self.prerelease = None` \u2014 but prerelease is now a property without a setter! That would raise AttributeError (can't set attribute). Hmm.\n\nActually, `parse` does `self.prerelease = None` or `self.prerelease = (prerelease[0], int(prerelease_num))`. If prerelease is a read-only property on the class, setting `self.prerelease` raises `AttributeError: can't set attribute` (or in Python 3.11+, property has no setter \u2192 AttributeError).\n\nOptions:\n1. Add a setter to the property that stores to `_prerelease`, and the getter returns flaky values.\n2. Don't call parse; set `self.version` directly. But `version` is a plain attribute, so we can do:\n\n```python\nclass Flaky(StrictVersion):\n    @property\n    def prerelease(self):\n        self._accesses += 1\n        return ('a', 1) if self._accesses == 1 else None\n```\n\nBut wait, `self._accesses` needs to be initialized. If we don't call `__init__` with parse... Actually `Version.__init__(vstring=None)` only calls parse if vstring is truthy. So `Flaky()` with no args \u2192 no parse \u2192 no prerelease setting. Then we set `self.version = (1, 0, 0)` manually. And `self._accesses = 0`.\n\nHmm, but the property getter accesses `self._accesses` \u2014 we need to initialize it before any access. Let's write:\n\n```python\ndef test_cmp_dead_code_branch():\n    class FlakyPrerelease(StrictVersion):\n        def __init__(self):\n            self.version = (1, 0, 0)\n            self._accesses = 0\n\n        @property\n        def prerelease(self):\n            self._accesses += 1\n            return ('a', 1) if self._accesses == 1 else None\n\n    v1 = FlakyPrerelease()\n    v2 = FlakyPrerelease()\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError, match=\"never get here\"):\n        v1._cmp(v2)\n```\n\nWait, but `StrictVersion` doesn't define `__init__`, so `FlakyPrerelease.__init__` overrides `Version.__init__`. That's fine.\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: `v1._cmp(v2)` \u2014 `self` is v1, `other` is v2. `isinstance(other, StrictVersion)` \u2192 FlakyPrerelease is a subclass \u2192 True. Good. `self.version != other.version` \u2192 (1,0,0) == (1,0,0) \u2192 False \u2192 skip. Then prerelease logic:\n\n- Cond 1: `not self.prerelease` \u2192 v1.prerelease access #1 \u2192 ('a',1) truthy \u2192 not \u2192 False. Short-circuit: `not other.prerelease` NOT evaluated. Cond 1 = False. \u2713\n- Cond 2: `self.prerelease` \u2192 v1 access #2 \u2192 None \u2192 falsy. Cond 2 = False. \u2713\n- Cond 3: `not self.prerelease` \u2192 v1 access #3 \u2192 None \u2192 not None = True. `other.prerelease` \u2192 v2 access #1 \u2192 ('a',1) \u2192 truthy. Cond 3 = True \u2192 return 1. \u2717\n\nOops! That returns 1, not reaching else. Let me redo the trace.\n\nI need: cond1 False, cond2 False, cond3 False, cond4 False.\n\nCond 1: `not self.prerel