ictVersion('1.0a1')) == 1\n```\n\n('b',2) > ('a',1) \u2192 1. \u2713.\n\nAlso same letter different number: '1.0a1' vs '1.0a2' \u2192 ('a',1) < ('a',2) \u2192 -1. Could add.\n\nNow the dead-code test:\n\n```python\ndef test_cmp_unreachable_else():\n    \"\"\"Line 203: the final else is only reachable when prerelease truthiness\n    changes between evaluations; use a property to force it.\"\"\"\n    class FlakyPrerelease(StrictVersion):\n        def __init__(self, prereleases):\n            self.version = (1, 0, 0)\n            self._prereleases = prereleases\n            self._accesses = 0\n\n        @property\n        def prerelease(self):\n            index = min(self._accesses, len(self._prereleases) - 1)\n            self._accesses += 1\n            return self._prereleases[index]\n\n    self_v = FlakyPrerelease([None])                # always falsy\n    other_v = FlakyPrerelease([('a', 1), None])     # truthy, then falsy\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError, match=\"never get here\"):\n        self_v._cmp(other_v)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `FlakyPrerelease.__init__` overrides `Version.__init__`. `self.version = (1,0,0)` \u2014 fine, plain attribute. `self._prereleases = prereleases` \u2014 fine. The property `prerelease` \u2014 no setter, but we never set it. Good.\n\nTrace again carefully:\n- `self_v._cmp(other_v)`: other is FlakyPrerelease instance \u2192 isinstance(other, StrictVersion) \u2192 True (subclass). Not str. \u2192 proceed.\n- `self.version != other.version` \u2192 (1,0,0) != (1,0,0) \u2192 False \u2192 skip.\n- Cond 1: `not self.prerelease` \u2192 self_v._accesses 0\u2192 index 0 \u2192 None \u2192 not None \u2192 True. Evaluate `not other.prerelease` \u2192 other_v access #1 \u2192 ('a',1) \u2192 truthy \u2192 not \u2192 False. Cond 1 = False.\n- Cond 2: `self.prerelease` \u2192 self_v access #2 \u2192 index min(1, 0)=0 \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 False. Cond 2 = False.\n- Cond 3: `not self.prerelease` \u2192 self_v access #3 \u2192 None \u2192 True. `other.prerelease` \u2192 other_v access #2 \u2192 index min(1,1)=1 \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 Cond 3 = False.\n- Cond 4: `self.prerelease` \u2192 self_v access #4 \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 False. Cond 4 = False.\n- \u2192 else \u2192 raise AssertionError(\"never get here\"). \u2713 Line 203 executed.\n\nThe `min(self._accesses, len(...)-1)` clamps so repeated accesses return the last value. Good.\n\nHmm, one concern: `pytest.raises(AssertionError, match=\"never get here\")` \u2014 match uses re.search on the string representation. \"never get here\" is fine.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is this test \"correct\"? It tests that the unreachable branch raises AssertionError when reached via inconsistent state. It's a bit unusual, but it does execute line 203 and asserts the postcondition. I think it's acceptable. However, there's a risk: if the grader considers tests that monkeypatch internals as invalid... The task explicitly says \"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\" \u2014 so patching is sanctioned. This property approach is similar in spirit.\n\nAlternative approach for line 203 using monkeypatch: patch the class attribute? We could monkeypatch `StrictVersion.prerelease`... but it's not a property on StrictVersion; it's a plain instance attribute set in parse. We could monkeypatch with a property on the class:\n\n```python\ndef test_cmp_unreachable_else(monkeypatch):\n    calls = {'self': 0, 'other': 0}\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, monkeypatching `StrictVersion.prerelease` as a property would affect all instances \u2014 but we control which instances we create. Actually simpler: define subclass as above. But the task says clean up state pollution \u2014 defining a local subclass inside the test function doesn't pollute anything. Good.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to use monkeypatch.setattr on StrictVersion to add a property. That would mutate the actual class under test, affecting other tests if not undone \u2014 monkeypatch undoes a