       if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c >= 0\n```\n\nSo I need to create a test subclass that implements `parse`, `_cmp`, and `__str__`.\n\nLet me use get_info to check the module structure first. Actually, the tool `get_info` returns information about a symbol. Let me query for `Version`, `StrictVersion`, `LooseVersion` to understand.\n\nActually, since the code is given, I can write tests directly. But the instructions say to use get_info as necessary. Let me check what's in the module to see if there are existing concrete classes I could use, or whether I should define my own minimal subclass.\n\nLet me call get_info on a few symbols to understand the module.\n\nI'll query:\n- `Version`\n- `StrictVersion`\n- `LooseVersion`\n\nThese are independent calls, so I can make them all at once.\n\nThen I'll write the test.\n\nPlan for tests:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version\n\n\nclass _MinimalVersion(Version):\n    \"\"\"Concrete subclass used only for exercising Version's shared logic.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None, cmp_result=0):\n        super().__init__(vstring)\n        self.cmp_result = cmp_result\n\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.vstring = vstring\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return getattr(self, \"vstring\", \"\")\n\n    def _cmp(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, _MinimalVersion):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.cmp_result\n```\n\nWait, but `_cmp` returning NotImplemented \u2014 the comparison methods then return NotImplemented, which Python interprets as \"not supported\" and falls back. When comparing with `==`, if both return NotImplemented, Python falls back to identity comparison, so `v == object()` would be False. To directly test the NotImplemented branch, I can call the dunder methods directly: `v.__eq__(object()) is NotImplemented`.\n\nTests:\n\n1. `test_init_with_vstring_parses`: create `_MinimalVersion(\"1.2.3\")`, assert `v.vstring == \"1.2.3\"` (covers lines 47-48).\n2. `test_init_without_vstring`: create `_MinimalVersion()`, assert no `vstring` attribute (covers the false branch of line 47).\n3. `test_repr`: `repr(_MinimalVersion(\"1.2.3\")) == \"_MinimalVersion ('1.2.3')\"` (covers line 51).\n4. Comparison tests: for each of `__eq__`, `__lt__`, `__le__`, `__gt__`, `__ge__`, test:\n   - NotImplemented path (call dunder directly with a non-_MinimalVersion object, assert result `is NotImplemented`)\n   - Normal path with cmp_result values -1, 0, 1.\n\nLet me write parametrized tests:\n\n```python\nimport operator\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"op,cmp_result,expected\", [\n    (operator.eq, 0, True),\n    (operator.eq, -1, False),\n    (operator.eq, 1, False),\n    (operator.lt, -1, True),\n    (operator.lt, 0, False),\n    (operator.lt, 1, False),\n    (operator.le, -1, True),\n    (operator.le, 0, True),\n    (operator.le, 1, False),\n    (operator.gt, 1, True),\n    (operator.gt, 0, False),\n    (operator.gt, -1, False),\n    (operator.ge, 1, True),\n    (operator.ge, 0, True),\n    (operator.ge, -1, False),\n])\ndef test_comparisons(op, cmp_result, expected):\n    v = _MinimalVersion(cmp_result=cmp_result)\n    other = _MinimalVersion()\n    assert op(v, other) is expected\n```\n\nWait \u2014 careful: `op(v, other)` with `operator.eq` calls `v.__eq__(other)`. If `_cmp` returns an int, then `c == 0` etc. works. But `assert op(v, other) is expected` \u2014 `c == 0` returns a bool, so `is True` works. Good.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety: `operator.eq(v, other)` calls `v.__eq__(other)`; if that returns NotImplemented (not the case here since other is _MinimalVersion), it would try reflected. Fine.\n\nFor the NotImplemented branch:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"op\", [operator.eq, operator.lt, operator.le, operator.gt, operator.ge])\ndef test_comparison_not_implemented(op):\n    v = _MinimalVersion()\n    sentinel = object()\n    # call the dunder directly so Python doesn't fall back to identity comparison\n    result = getattr(v, \"__%s__\" % op.__name__)(sentinel)\n    assert result is NotImplemented\n```\n\nHmm, `operator.eq.__name__` is \"eq\", so `\"__eq__\"`. That works. But maybe clearer to just write it explicitly:\n\n```python\ndef test_comparisons_return_not_implemented_for_foreign_types():\n    v = _MinimalVersion()\n    other = object()\n    assert v.__eq__(other) is NotImplemented\n    assert v.__lt__(other) is NotImplemented\n    assert v.__le__(other) is NotImplemented\n    assert v.__gt__(other) is NotImplemented\n    assert v.__ge__(other) is NotImplemented\n```\n\nThat's simple and clear.\n\nAlso, when using `==` with a foreign object: `v == object()` \u2192 `v.__eq__(obj)` returns NotImplemented \u2192 Python tries `object().__eq__(v)` which returns NotImplemented \u2192 falls back to identity \u2192 False. So `assert (v == object()) is False`. That's a good end-to-end check too.\n\nNow, about the import: the module is `ansible.module_utils.compat.version`. Let me verify with get_info. Actually, the get_info tool takes a symbol name like 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo in class C. It probably searches the current module context. Hmm, it's ambiguous what module context it uses. Let me try calling it with names like `Version`, `StrictVersion`, `LooseVersion` to see what it returns.\n\nActually, I should be careful: the get_info tool likely operates on the code under test context. Let me just call it and see.\n\nAlso need to consider: does `Version` have abstract methods that raise NotImplementedError? In the original distutils version.py:\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n    ...\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        raise NotImplementedError ...\n```\n\nWait no. In the original distutils version.py, `Version` doesn't define comparison methods; `StrictVersion` and `LooseVersion` define `_cmp`. Actually in newer Python versions (3.10+?), distutils' Version got the rich comparison routing. Let me recall...\n\nActually in Python 3.12's distutils (removed) and in setuptools' vendored version, the `Version` base class has:\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n    \"\"\"Abstract base class for version numbering classes.  Just provides\n    constructor (__init__) and reproducer (__repr__), because those\n    seem to be the same for all version numbering classes; and route\n    rich comparisons to _cmp.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c == 0\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c < 0\n\n    def __le__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c <= 0\n\n    def __gt__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c > 0\n\n    def __ge__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c >= 0\n```\n\nYes, that matches the given code. And `parse` and `_cmp` are abstract-ish (raise NotImplementedError in subclasses? No \u2014 in distutils, `StrictVersion.parse` etc. are defined; the base class just doesn't define them, so calling them raises AttributeError).\n\nAnsible's compat version module: `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py` \u2014 this was added to provide `LooseVersion` since distutils was removed in Python 3.12. It contains `Version`, `StrictVersion`, and `LooseVersion`.\n\nSo I could use `LooseVersion` for testing, but the NotImplemented branch of `_cmp` \u2014 LooseVersion's `_cmp`:\n\n```python\n    def _cmp(self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, String):\n            other = LooseVersion(other)\n        if self.version == other.version:\n            return 0\n        if self.version < other.version:\n            return -1\n        if self.version > other.version:\n            return 1\n```\n\nIt never returns NotImplemented. So to cover the NotImplemented branches (55-56, 61-62, etc.), I need a custom subclass whose `_cmp` returns NotImplemented. So a custom minimal subclass is the way to go.\n\nAlternatively, use LooseVersion for the normal paths and a custom subclass for NotImplemented paths. But a single custom subclass covers everything cleanly. However, using the real classes adds integration value. Let me do both: use LooseVersion for the parse/repr/normal comparison tests (real-world usage), and a custom subclass for the NotImplemented branches.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 LooseVersion's `__init__` is overridden:\n\n```python\nclass LooseVersion(Version):\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n```\n\nActually LooseVersion overrides `__init__`? Let me recall distutils LooseVersion:\n\n```python\nclass LooseVersion(Version):\n    ...\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None):\n        self.parse(vstring)\n```\n\nHmm, in distutils:\n\n```python\nclass LooseVersion (Version):\n\n    \"\"\"Version numbering for anarchists and software realists.\n    ...\n    \"\"\"\n\n    component_re = re.compile(r'(\\d+ | [a-zA-Z]+ | \\. | -)')\n\n    def __init__ (self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __str__ (self):\n        return self.vstring\n\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"LooseVersion ('%s')\" % str(self)\n\n    def parse (self, vstring):\n        self.vstring = vstring\n        components = [x for x in self.component_re.split(vstring)\n                              if x and x != '.' and x != '-']\n        for i, obj in enumerate(components):\n            try:\n                components[i] = int(obj)\n            except ValueError:\n                pass\n        self.version = components\n\n    def _cmp (self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, String):\n            other = LooseVersion(other)\n        if self.version == other.version:\n            return 0\n        if self.version < other.version:\n            return -1\n        if self.version > other.version:\n            return 1\n```\n\nLooseVersion overrides `__repr__` too. So using LooseVersion wouldn't cover line 51 (Version.__repr__). StrictVersion also overrides `__repr__`:\n\n```python\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"StrictVersion ('%s')\" % str(self)\n```\n\nSo neither subclass covers Version.__repr__ line 51. Hence a custom subclass is needed for line 51 anyway.\n\nAlso, LooseVersion._cmp can return None implicitly if none of the conditions match? Actually if versions are equal it returns 0; less returns -1; greater returns 1. Since any two lists compare one of <, ==, >, it always returns. OK.\n\nBut wait \u2014 comparing LooseVersion with a non-string, non-LooseVersion object: `other.version` raises AttributeError. So `LooseVersion('1.0') == object()` raises AttributeError, not returning NotImplemented. So the NotImplemented branches definitely need a custom subclass.\n\nDecision: define a minimal concrete subclass in the test module. Use it for all tests. Optionally also test with LooseVersion for realism, but that's optional. Keep it focused.\n\nNow, about imports and cleanup: The test doesn't create files or modify global state, so cleanup is minimal. No monkeypatching needed really. But the instructions say \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since we don't mutate any global state, nothing to clean. Defining a module-level subclass is fine and doesn't pollute the module under test.\n\nLet me now check the module with get_info to confirm the import path and class names. The tool signature: `get_info(name)` \u2014 \"Returns information about a symbol.\" I'll query `Version`, `LooseVersion`, `StrictVersion`.\n\nLet me make the calls.\n\nAfter that, write the test file.\n\nTest file structure:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for ansible.module_utils.compat.version.Version shared logic.\"\"\"\n\nimport operator\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version\n\n\nclass _ComparableVersion(Version):\n    \"\"\"Minimal concrete Version used to exercise the base-class logic.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None, cmp_result=0):\n        self.cmp_result = cmp_result\n        super().__init__(vstring)\n\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.vstring = vstring\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return getattr(self, \"vstring\", \"\")\n\n    def _cmp(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, _ComparableVersion):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.cmp_result\n\n\ndef test_init_parses_vstring():\n    v = _ComparableVersion(\"1.2.3\")\n    assert v.vstring == \"1.2.3\"\n\n\ndef test_init_without_vstring_skips_parse():\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert not hasattr(v, \"vstring\")\n\n\ndef test_repr():\n    v = _ComparableVersion(\"1.2.3\")\n    assert repr(v) == \"_ComparableVersion ('1.2.3')\"\n\n\ndef test_repr_empty():\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert repr(v) == \"_ComparableVersion ('')\"\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"op,result,expected\", [...])\ndef test_rich_comparisons(op, result, expected):\n    ...\n\n\ndef test_comparisons_return_not_implemented():\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, careful with `test_init_without_vstring_skips_parse`: `Version.__init__` with `vstring=None` \u2014 `if vstring:` is False, so parse not called. But my subclass's `__init__` sets `self.cmp_result` before calling super. Good.\n\nHmm, one subtlety: in `_ComparableVersion.__init__`, I set `cmp_result` before `super().__init__(vstring)`. If `parse` were called and raised, cmp_result would still be set. Fine.\n\nFor the parametrized comparison test:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"op\", \"cmp_result\", \"expected\"),\n    [\n        (operator.eq, -1, False),\n        (operator.eq, 0, True),\n        (operator.eq, 1, False),\n        (operator.lt, -1, True),\n        (operator.lt, 0, False),\n        (operator.lt, 1, False),\n        (operator.le, -1, True),\n        (operator.le, 0, True),\n        (operator.le, 1, False),\n        (operator.gt, -1, False),\n        (operator.gt, 0, False),\n        (operator.gt, 1, True),\n        (operator.ge, -1, False),\n        (operator.ge, 0, True),\n        (operator.ge, 1, True),\n    ],\n)\ndef test_rich_comparisons(op, cmp_result, expected):\n    left = _ComparableVersion(cmp_result=cmp_result)\n    right = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert op(left, right) is expected\n```\n\nNote: `op(left, right)` \u2014 `operator.eq(left, right)` calls `type(left).__eq__(left, right)`? Actually operator.eq calls the tp_richcompare slot, which for a Python class with `__eq__` defined calls it. Since `_cmp` returns an int, `__eq__` returns a bool. `is expected` works since bools are singletons.\n\nBut careful: `operator.eq(left, right)` \u2014 if `left.__eq__(right)` returned NotImplemented, Python's operator.eq would... actually `operator.eq` is `PyObject_RichCompare` which handles the reflected fallback. But here it returns a bool, so fine.\n\nFor the NotImplemented branch, calling the dunder directly:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    \"dunder\",\n    [\"__eq__\", \"__lt__\", \"__le__\", \"__gt__\", \"__ge__\"],\n)\ndef test_comparisons_with_unsupported_type_return_not_implemented(dunder):\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    result = getattr(v, dunder)(object())\n    assert result is NotImplemented\n```\n\nAnd also verify end-to-end behavior with operators:\n\n```python\ndef test_eq_with_unsupported_type_falls_back_to_identity():\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    other = object()\n    assert (v == other) is False\n    assert (v != other) is True\n```\n\nHmm, `v != other`: `__ne__` is not defined in Version; Python's default `__ne__` (object's) delegates to `__eq__` and inverts unless NotImplemented. Since `__eq__` returns NotImplemented, `object.__ne__` fallback... Actually the default `__ne__` in Python 3: if `__eq__` returns NotImplemented, then it tries the reflected `other.__ne__(v)`... object doesn't define `__ne__` meaningfully; the default behavior returns the inverse of identity comparison result. So `v != other` \u2192 True since they're different objects. OK, that assertion is safe. But maybe keep it simple and skip the `!=` check to avoid subtle issues. Actually it's fine: `v == other` is False (identity fallback), `v != other` is True. I'll include just the `==` one to be safe.\n\nAlso, should I test with the real `LooseVersion` to make sure the base-class comparisons work in real usage? That could be a nice integration test:\n\n```python\ndef test_loose_version_comparisons_via_base_class():\n    from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import LooseVersion\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") < LooseVersion(\"1.1\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") <= LooseVersion(\"1.0\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"2.0\") > LooseVersion(\"1.9\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") >= LooseVersion(\"1.0\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") == LooseVersion(\"1.0\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"\n```\n\nWait \u2014 does `LooseVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"` work? `_cmp` converts string to LooseVersion, returns 0, `__eq__` returns True. Yes.\n\nBut careful: does ansible's compat version module have LooseVersion? Let me check with get_info. Also StrictVersion.\n\nActually, wait. There's a subtlety with `LooseVersion(\"1.0\") < LooseVersion(\"1.1\")`: `_cmp` compares `self.version < other.version` where version is `[1, 0]` vs `[1, 1]` \u2192 -1 \u2192 `__lt__` returns True. Good.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 does LooseVersion in ansible's compat module define its own `__lt__` etc.? In distutils, LooseVersion doesn't define rich comparisons; it relies on `_cmp`? Actually no \u2014 in old distutils, LooseVersion defined `__cmp__`? Let me recall. Old distutils version.py (Python 2 era):\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        ...\n\n    def __cmp__(self, other):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n```\n\nHmm, actually old distutils Version had `__cmp__` raising NotImplementedError, and StrictVersion/LooseVersion implemented `__cmp__`. Then in Python 3, `__cmp__` was removed... The distutils version.py in Python 3.x:\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n    \"\"\"Abstract base class for version numbering classes.  Just provides\n    constructor (__init__) and reproducer (__repr__), because those\n    seem to be the same for all version numbering classes.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__ (self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        raise NotImplementedError ...\n\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me think about the setuptools vendored version (setuptools._distutils.version). In setuptools' vendored distutils, `version.py` was deprecated and later they made LooseVersion a wrapper around packaging? Actually setuptools deprecated LooseVersion.\n\nThe code given in the prompt matches what I recall from newer distutils (Python 3.10+?) where they added rich comparison routing to `_cmp` in the base class. Let me check: Python 3.10's distutils version.py:\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n    \"\"\"Abstract base class for version numbering classes.  Just provides\n    constructor (__init__) and reproducer (__repr__), because those\n    seem to be the same for all version numbering classes.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__ (self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n```\n\nHmm, that's the older one without comparisons. The one with `_cmp` routing... I recall a change where `LooseVersion` comparison with different types raised TypeError, and there was bpo-... Actually, I remember in Python 3.12's distutils was removed, and setuptools vendored it. The setuptools vendored `version.py` at some point:\n\nLooking at setuptools/_distutils/version.py (current):\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n    \"\"\"Abstract base class for version numbering classes.  Just provides\n    constructor (__init__) and reproducer (__repr__), because those\n    seem to be the same for all version numbering classes; and route\n    rich comparisons to _cmp.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c == 0\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c < 0\n\n    def __le__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c <= 0\n\n    def __gt__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c > 0\n\n    def __ge__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c >= 0\n\n    # subclass must implement __lt__ etc... no\n```\n\nYes, this matches. And ansible copied this file into `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py`. Ansible-core added this in... ansible-core 2.17? They vendored distutils version for LooseVersion since distutils removal. Yes, `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py` exists in ansible-core with `Version`, `StrictVersion`, `LooseVersion`.\n\nSo the import path `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version, LooseVersion` should work.\n\nNow, does LooseVersion in that file define `__str__`? Yes:\n\n```python\n    def __str__ (self):\n        return self.vstring\n```\n\nAnd LooseVersion overrides `__repr__`? In distutils LooseVersion:\n\n```python\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"LooseVersion ('%s')\" % str(self)\n```\n\nYes. So Version.__repr__ (line 51) is only reachable via a subclass that doesn't override `__repr__`. My custom subclass handles that.\n\nNow let me also double check `Version.__init__` line 47-48: `if vstring: self.parse(vstring)`. LooseVersion(\"1.0\") calls its own `__init__` which is identical. StrictVersion too. So to cover Version.__init__ lines, must instantiate Version subclass calling super().__init__ or Version itself? Version itself can be instantiated (it's not an ABC with abstractmethod decorators \u2014 it's \"abstract\" only by convention). `Version(\"1.0\")` would call `self.parse(\"1.0\")` \u2192 AttributeError since Version has no parse. So can't instantiate Version directly with a vstring. `Version()` works (no parse call). So my custom subclass is needed.\n\nNow, the get_info tool: I should use it \"as necessary\". Let me query the module symbols to confirm names and behavior. I'll call get_info for `Version`, `LooseVersion`, `StrictVersion` in one block since they're independent.\n\nActually, what module does get_info search? Probably the module under test (the file from which the code was extracted). Let me try.\n\nThen write the final test.\n\nOne more consideration: the test file should be self-contained and importable. The import `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version` \u2014 if tests run in the ansible repo, that works. I'll use that import path.\n\nAlso,