ion for LooseVersion since distutils removal. Yes, `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py` exists in ansible-core with `Version`, `StrictVersion`, `LooseVersion`.\n\nSo the import path `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version, LooseVersion` should work.\n\nNow, does LooseVersion in that file define `__str__`? Yes:\n\n```python\n    def __str__ (self):\n        return self.vstring\n```\n\nAnd LooseVersion overrides `__repr__`? In distutils LooseVersion:\n\n```python\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"LooseVersion ('%s')\" % str(self)\n```\n\nYes. So Version.__repr__ (line 51) is only reachable via a subclass that doesn't override `__repr__`. My custom subclass handles that.\n\nNow let me also double check `Version.__init__` line 47-48: `if vstring: self.parse(vstring)`. LooseVersion(\"1.0\") calls its own `__init__` which is identical. StrictVersion too. So to cover Version.__init__ lines, must instantiate Version subclass calling super().__init__ or Version itself? Version itself can be instantiated (it's not an ABC with abstractmethod decorators \u2014 it's \"abstract\" only by convention). `Version(\"1.0\")` would call `self.parse(\"1.0\")` \u2192 AttributeError since Version has no parse. So can't instantiate Version directly with a vstring. `Version()` works (no parse call). So my custom subclass is needed.\n\nNow, the get_info tool: I should use it \"as necessary\". Let me query the module symbols to confirm names and behavior. I'll call get_info for `Version`, `LooseVersion`, `StrictVersion` in one block since they're independent.\n\nActually, what module does get_info search? Probably the module under test (the file from which the code was extracted). Let me try.\n\nThen write the final test.\n\nOne more consideration: the test file should be self-contained and importable. The import `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version` \u2014 if tests run in the ansible repo, that works. I'll use that import path.\n\nAlso, to be