module with get_info to confirm the import path and class names. The tool signature: `get_info(name)` \u2014 \"Returns information about a symbol.\" I'll query `Version`, `LooseVersion`, `StrictVersion`.\n\nLet me make the calls.\n\nAfter that, write the test file.\n\nTest file structure:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for ansible.module_utils.compat.version.Version shared logic.\"\"\"\n\nimport operator\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version\n\n\nclass _ComparableVersion(Version):\n    \"\"\"Minimal concrete Version used to exercise the base-class logic.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None, cmp_result=0):\n        self.cmp_result = cmp_result\n        super().__init__(vstring)\n\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.vstring = vstring\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return getattr(self, \"vstring\", \"\")\n\n    def _cmp(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, _ComparableVersion):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.cmp_result\n\n\ndef test_init_parses_vstring():\n    v = _ComparableVersion(\"1.2.3\")\n    assert v.vstring == \"1.2.3\"\n\n\ndef test_init_without_vstring_skips_parse():\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert not hasattr(v, \"vstring\")\n\n\ndef test_repr():\n    v = _ComparableVersion(\"1.2.3\")\n    assert repr(v) == \"_ComparableVersion ('1.2.3')\"\n\n\ndef test_repr_empty():\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert repr(v) == \"_ComparableVersion ('')\"\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"op,result,expected\", [...])\ndef test_rich_comparisons(op, result, expected):\n    ...\n\n\ndef test_comparisons_return_not_implemented():\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, careful with `test_init_without_vstring_skips_parse`: `Version.__init__` with `vstring=None` \u2014 `if vstring:` is False, so parse not called. But my subclass's `__init__` sets `self.cmp_result` before calling super. Good.\n\nHmm, one subtlety: in `_ComparableVersion.__init__`, I set `cmp_result` before `super().__init__(vstring)`. If `parse` were called and raised, cmp_result would still be set. Fine.\n\nFor the parametrized comparison test:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"op\", \"cmp_result\", \"expected\"),\n    [\n        (operator.eq, -1, False),\n        (operator.eq, 0, True),\n        (operator.eq, 1, False),\n        (operator.lt, -1, True),\n        (operator.lt, 0, False),\n        (operator.lt, 1, False),\n        (operator.le, -1, True),\n        (operator.le, 0, True),\n        (operator.le, 1, False),\n        (operator.gt, -1, False),\n        (operator.gt, 0, False),\n        (operator.gt, 1, True),\n        (operator.ge, -1, False),\n        (operator.ge, 0, True),\n        (operator.ge, 1, True),\n    ],\n)\ndef test_rich_comparisons(op, cmp_result, expected):\n    left = _ComparableVersion(cmp_result=cmp_result)\n    right = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert op(left, right) is expected\n```\n\nNote: `op(left, right)` \u2014 `operator.eq(left, right)` calls `type(left).__eq__(left, right)`? Actually operator.eq calls the tp_richcompare slot, which for a Python class with `__eq__` defined calls it. Since `_cmp` returns an int, `__eq__` returns a bool. `is expected` works since bools are singletons.\n\nBut careful: `operator.eq(left, right)` \u2014 if `left.__eq__(right)` returned NotImplemented, Python's operator.eq would... actually `operator.eq` is `PyObject_RichCompare` which handles the reflected fallback. But here it returns a bool, so fine.\n\nFor the NotImplemented branch, calling the dunder directly:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    \"dunder\",\n    [\"__eq__\", \"__lt__\", \"__le__\", \"__gt__\", \"__ge__\"],\n)\ndef test_comparisons_with_unsupported_type_return_not_implemented(dunder):\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    result = getattr(v, dunder)(object())\n    assert result is NotImplemented\n```\n\nAnd also verify end-to-end behavior with operators:\n\n```python\ndef test_eq_with_unsupported_type_falls_back_to_identity():\n    v