n for LooseVersion since distutils removal. Yes, `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py` exists in ansible-core with `Version`, `StrictVersion`, `LooseVersion`.\n\nSo the import path `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version, LooseVersion` should work.\n\nNow, does LooseVersion in that file define `__str__`? Yes:\n\n```python\n    def __str__ (self):\n        return self.vstring\n```\n\nAnd LooseVersion overrides `__repr__`? In distutils LooseVersion:\n\n```python\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"LooseVersion ('%s')\" % str(self)\n```\n\nYes. So Version.__repr__ (line 51) is only reachable via a subclass that doesn't override `__repr__`. My custom subclass handles that.\n\nNow let me also double check `Version.__init__` line 47-48: `if vstring: self.parse(vstring)`. LooseVersion(\"1.0\") calls its own `__init__` which is identical. StrictVersion too. So to cover Version.__init__ lines, must instantiate Version subclass calling super().__init__ or Version itself? Version itself can be instantiated (it's not an ABC with abstractmethod decorators \u2014 it's \"abstract\" only by convention). `Version(\"1.0\")` would call `self.parse(\"1.0\")` \u2192 AttributeError since Version has no parse. So can't instantiate Version directly with a vstring. `Version()` works (no parse call). So my custom subclass is needed.\n\nNow, the get_info tool: I should use it \"as necessary\". Let me query the module symbols to confirm names and behavior. I'll call get_info for `Version`, `LooseVersion`, `StrictVersion` in one block since they're independent.\n\nActually, what module does get_info search? Probably the module under test (the file from which the code was extracted). Let me try.\n\nThen write the final test.\n\nOne more consideration: the test file should be self-contained and importable. The import `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import Version` \u2014 if tests run in the ansible repo, that works. I'll use that import path.\n\nAlso, to be safe about `__str__` for my subclass when vstring wasn't set: `getattr(self, \"vstring\", \"\")`. Fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether `test_init_without_vstring` should assert something stronger \u2014 that parse wasn't called. I could track parse calls:\n\n```python\nclass _ComparableVersion(Version):\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None, cmp_result=0):\n        self.parse_calls = []\n        self.cmp_result = cmp_result\n        super().__init__(vstring)\n\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.parse_calls.append(vstring)\n        self.vstring = vstring\n```\n\nThen `test_init_without_vstring`: `assert v.parse_calls == []`. And `test_init_with_vstring`: `assert v.parse_calls == [\"1.2.3\"]`. That's a stronger postcondition. \n\nNow the full test script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests targeting the uncovered lines/branches of\nansible.module_utils.compat.version.Version.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport operator\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.compat.version import LooseVersion, Version\n\n\nclass _ComparableVersion(Version):\n    \"\"\"Minimal concrete Version used to drive the base-class logic.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None, cmp_result=0):\n        self.parse_calls = []\n        self.cmp_result = cmp_result\n        super().__init__(vstring)\n\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.parse_calls.append(vstring)\n        self.vstring = vstring\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return getattr(self, \"vstring\", \"\")\n\n    def _cmp(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, _ComparableVersion):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.cmp_result\n\n\ndef test_init_with_vstring_calls_parse():\n    v = _ComparableVersion(\"1.2.3\")\n    assert v.parse_calls == [\"1.2.3\"]\n    assert v.vstring == \"1.2.3\"\n\n\ndef test_init_without_vstring_does_not_parse():\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert v.parse_calls == []\n    assert not hasattr(v, \"vstring\")\n\n\ndef test_repr_uses_class_name_and_str():\n    assert repr(_ComparableVersion(\"1.2.3\")) == \"_ComparableVersion ('1.2.3')\"\n    assert repr(_ComparableVersion()) == \"_ComparableVersion ('')\"\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"op\", \"cmp_result\", \"expected\"),\n    [\n        (operator.eq, -1, False),\n        (operator.eq, 0, True),\n        (operator.eq, 1, False),\n        (operator.lt, -1, True),\n        (operator.lt, 0, False),\n        (operator.lt, 1, False),\n        (operator.le, -1, True),\n        (operator.le, 0, True),\n        (operator.le, 1, False),\n        (operator.gt, -1, False),\n        (operator.gt, 0, False),\n        (operator.gt, 1, True),\n        (operator.ge, -1, False),\n        (operator.ge, 0, True),\n        (operator.ge, 1, True),\n    ],\n)\ndef test_rich_comparisons_route_to_cmp(op, cmp_result, expected):\n    left = _ComparableVersion(cmp_result=cmp_result)\n    right = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert op(left, right) is expected\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"dunder\", [\"__eq__\", \"__lt__\", \"__le__\", \"__gt__\", \"__ge__\"])\ndef test_comparisons_propagate_not_implemented(dunder):\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    assert getattr(v, dunder)(object()) is NotImplemented\n\n\ndef test_eq_against_unsupported_type_falls_back():\n    v = _ComparableVersion()\n    other = object()\n    assert (v == other) is False\n\n\ndef test_loose_version_uses_inherited_comparisons():\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") < LooseVersion(\"1.1\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") <= LooseVersion(\"1.0\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"2.0\") > LooseVersion(\"1.9\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") >= LooseVersion(\"1.0\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") == LooseVersion(\"1.0\")\n    assert LooseVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"\n    assert (LooseVersion(\"1.0\") == LooseVersion(\"2.0\")) is False\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `LooseVersion(\"1.0\") == \"1.0\"`: `_cmp` converts \"1.0\" to LooseVersion, versions [1, 0] == [1, 0] \u2192 0 \u2192 True. Good.\n\nHmm, but does ansible's compat LooseVersion handle strin