. If the instance has no `__str__`, then `str(self)` calls `type(self).__repr__(self)` \u2192 infinite recursion \u2192 `RecursionError`.\n\nHmm, but in Python 3, the default `object.__str__` calls `object.__repr__`. So `str(self)` \u2192 `object.__str__(self)` \u2192 calls `repr(self)` \u2192 `Version.__repr__(self)` \u2192 `str(self)` \u2192 ... infinite recursion. Yes, that's a problem.\n\nSo for the mock subclass, I need to define `__str__` too, or rely on `LooseVersion` which has `__str__`. So better: call `Version.__repr__(loose_instance)` directly, or create a subclass that inherits `__str__`.\n\nOption: \n```python\nclass _StrVersion(Version):\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.vstring = vstring\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.vstring\n```\nThen `repr(_StrVersion('1.2'))` \u2192 `\"_StrVersion ('1.2')\"`. \n\nAlternatively use `Version.__repr__(LooseVersion('1.2.3'))` which returns `\"Version ('1.2.3')\"`. Hmm, `self.__class__.__name__` would be \"LooseVersion\". Either works. I'll use a subclass to keep it clean, or call the unbound method. Both are fine. Let me use a subclass with `parse` and `__str__` defined \u2014 that hits lines 47-48 and 51 naturally.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the instruction says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". Using a small test subclass is a standard approach for testing abstract base classes. I'll do that.\n\n3. Lines 54-57 (`__eq__`): Need to test both branches:\n   - `c == 0` path: `LooseVersion('1.2.3') == LooseVersion('1.2.3')` \u2192 True. But wait, does `LooseVersion` inherit `__eq__` from `Version`? The get_info output for LooseVersion showed `__init__`, `parse`, `__str__`, `__repr__`, `_cmp` \u2014 it doesn't list `__eq__` etc., so rich comparisons are inherited from `Version`. \n   - `NotImplemented` path: `LooseVersion('1.2.3') == 42` \u2192 `LooseVersion._cmp(42)` returns `NotImplemented` (since 42 is not str and not LooseVersion) \u2192 `Version.__eq__` returns `NotImplemented`. Then Python falls back to identity comparison \u2192 False for `==`. To check the return value is `NotImplemented`, I can call the dunder directly: `LooseVersion('1.2').__eq__(42) is NotImplemented`.\n\n4. Lines 60-63 (`__lt__`): \n   - `LooseVersion('1.2') < LooseVersion('1.3')` \u2192 True (c=-1, -1 < 0).\n   - `LooseVersion('1.3') < LooseVersion('1.2')` \u2192 False (c=1).\n   - NotImplemented path: `LooseVersion('1.2').__lt__(42) is NotImplemented`.\n   Also `LooseVersion('1.2') < 42` \u2192 TypeError since right operand doesn't implement reflected... Actually `<` with NotImplemented from left: Python tries reflected `__gt__` of int, which returns NotImplemented, then raises TypeError. I can assert `pytest.raises(TypeError)`. But for branch coverage of lines 61-62, calling the dunder directly is most explicit. I'll do both: use operators for real comparisons and direct dunder calls for NotImplemented.\n\n5. Lines 66-69 (`__le__`): similar.\n6. Lines 72-75 (`__gt__`): similar.\n7. Lines 78-81 (`__ge__`): similar.\n\nAlso `StrictVersion` could be used, e.g., `StrictVersion('1.0') < StrictVersion('1.1')`, and `StrictVersion('1.0') == '1.0'` (string other \u2192 converted). Also prerelease comparisons: `StrictVersion('1.0a1') < StrictVersion('1.0')` \u2192 -1. These go through `Version.__eq__/__lt__` etc.\n\nFor `__eq__` NotImplemented: `StrictVersion('1.0').__eq__(42)` \u2192 NotImplemented.\n\nLet me also make sure cleanup: no state pollution \u2014 these tests don't modify module state, no monkeypatching needed. But the instruction says \"Be sure to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since we don't mutate anything, no cleanup is needed; the test subclass defined at module level is fine, or define it inside the test function to avoid global namespace pollution. I'll define helper subclasses inside the test functions to minimize top-level code. Actually defining them at module level is conventional and fine, but the instruction says \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 test functions and module-level imports are fine. I'll define the small helper class inside each test where needed, or one at module level. Let me define it inside tests to keep it self-contained.\n\nImport: The module is `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py`. The import path would be `ansible.module_utils.compat.version`. Typical imports in ansible compat modules: `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import LooseVersion, StrictVersion, Version`. I'll use that.\n\nLet me now write the tests:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for ansible.module_utils.compat.version.Version base-class behaviour.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.compat.version import LooseVersion, StrictVersion, Version\n\n\nclass _RecordingVersion(Version):\n    \"\"\"Minimal concrete Version that records parse() calls and supports str().\"\"\"\n\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.parsed = vstring\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.parsed\n```\n\nHmm, should the helper class be module-level or in-function? Module-level helper classes are fine and idiomatic. But \"as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 a class definition is top-level code. I'll define it inside the test functions that need it to be safe. Actually, defining a class inside a function is fine in Python.\n\nTest 1: constructor parses when vstring given, doesn't when omitted:\n\n```python\ndef test_init_with_vstring_calls_parse():\n    class RecordingVersion(Version):\n        def parse(self, vstring):\n            self.parsed = vstring\n\n    v = RecordingVersion('1.2.3')\n    assert v.parsed == '1.2.3'\n\n\ndef test_init_without_vstring_skips_parse():\n    class RecordingVersion(Version):\n        def parse(self, vstring):\n            raise AssertionError('parse must not be called')\n\n    v = RecordingVersion()\n    assert v.parsed is None if False else True  # hmm\n```\n\nBetter: \n```python\ndef test_init_without_vstring_skips_parse():\n    class RecordingVersion(Version):\n        parsed = 'untouched'\n        def parse(self, vstring):\n            self.parsed = vstring\n\n    v = RecordingVersion()\n    assert v.parsed == 'untouched'\n    v2 = RecordingVersion(vstring=None)\n    assert v2.parsed == 'untouched'\n```\n\nWait \u2014 but the coverage report says line 47 doesn't execute at all. If existing tests never call `Version.__init__`, then `RecordingVersion()` (no args) would execute line 47 with falsy vstring, and `RecordingVersion('1.2.3')` executes lines 47-48. Good \u2014 both branches covered.\n\nTest 2: `__repr__`:\n\n```python\ndef test_repr():\n    v = RecordingVersion('1.2.3')  # need parse to set vstring for __str__\n```\n\nWith `_RecordingVersion` where `__str__` returns `self.parsed`, `repr(v)` \u2192 `\"RecordingVersion ('1.2.3')\"`. Since the class name is local, the assertion would use `v.__class__.__name__`. Let me assert:\n\n```python\ndef test_base_repr():\n    class StrVersion(Version):\n        def parse(self, vstring):\n            self.vstring = vstring\n        def __str__(self):\n            return self.vstring\n\n    v = StrVersion('1.2.3')\n    assert repr(v) == \"%s ('1.2.3')\" % v.__class__.__name__\n```\n\nAlternatively use `Version.__repr__(LooseVersion('1.2.3'))` which returns `\"LooseVersion ('1.2.3')\"`. That also hits line 51 and uses a real class. Both hit the line. I might do both in one test. Let me use the real LooseVersion via unbound call to avoid needing a custom class:\n\n```python\ndef test_base_repr():\n    lv = LooseVersion('1.2.3')\n    assert Version.__repr__(lv) == \"LooseVersion ('1.2.3')\"\n```\n\nThat's simple and hits line 51. \n\nSimilarly for `__init__`:\n\n```python\ndef test_base_init_parses_vstring():\n    lv = LooseVersion('1.2.3')  # calls LooseVersion.__init__, not Version's\n    Version.__init__(lv, '1.2.3')  # explicitly invoke base init -> parse\n    assert lv.version == [1, 2, 3]\n```\n\nHmm, `Version.__init__(lv, '1.2.3')` \u2014 this calls `self.parse(vstring)` where self is the LooseVersion instance \u2192 `LooseVersion.parse` \u2192 sets `self.vstring` and `self.version`. That hits lines 47-48. And `Version.__init__(lv)` (no vstring) hits line 47 with falsy. That avoids defining custom classes entirely. \n\nBut is calling `Version.__init__(lv)` weird? It's legitimate for hitting the branch. Alternatively use a subclass. I think using real classes via explicit base-class invocation is cleaner and less contrived than defining stub subclasses... Actually, both are common. Let me combine: use a real subclass approach for init/parse semantics AND real LooseVersion for comparisons.\n\nActually, simplest robust plan:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.compat.version import LooseVersion, StrictVersion, Version\n\n\ndef test_version_init_parses_when_vstring_given():\n    \"\"\"Version.__init__ must call self.parse() for a truthy vstring (lines 47-48).\"\"\"\n    lv = LooseVersion.__new__(LooseVersion)   # bypass LooseVersion.__init__\n    Version.__init__(lv, '1.2.3')             # -> Version.__init__ lines 47-48\n    assert lv.vstring == '1.2.3'\n    assert lv.version == [1, 2, 3]\n\n\ndef test_version_init_noop_without_vstring():\n    \"\"\"Version.__init__ with no/None vstring must not call parse (line 47, false branch).\"\"\"\n    lv = LooseVersion.__new__(LooseVersion)\n    Version.__init__(lv)                      # vstring is None -> no parse call\n    assert not hasattr(lv, 'vstring')\n    assert not hasattr(lv, 'version')\n```\n\nHmm, `LooseVersion.__new__(LooseVersion)` \u2014 LooseVersion doesn't define `__new__`, so it's `object.__new__`. Fine.\n\nAlternatively simpler: `lv = LooseVersion(None)` \u2014 LooseVersion's own `__init__` also has `if vstring: self.parse(...)`, so `LooseVersion(None)` leaves it without vstring. But that doesn't hit `Version.__init__` line 47. The untested line is in `Version.__init__`, so I must invoke `Version.__init__` itself. Using `Version.__init__(lv, ...)` does that.\n\nOr use a subclass:\n\n```python\nclass _TaggingVersion(Version):\n    def parse(self, vstring):\n        self.parsed = vstring\n```\n\n`_TaggingVersion('1.2.3')` hits lines 47-48 through normal instantiation \u2014 more natural. And `_TaggingVersion()` hits line 47 false branch. This is cleaner. But it's a module-level class definition. To minimize top-level code, define inside the test. I'll define inside the test functions. Actually, one class used by two tests \u2014 could use a fixture, but simplest is defining in each test or one module-level. The instruction says \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 I think module-level class helper is acceptable; but to be safe, I'll define classes within test functions. Hmm, but then each test that needs it defines its own. Only 2 tests need it (init tests) plus repr test. Let me just use the `LooseVersion.__new__` + `Version.__init__` approach \u2014 no custom classes needed at all. Actually wait, for `__repr__` I need `str(self)` to work \u2014 LooseVersion has `__str__`, so `Version.__repr__(loose_instance)` works fine.\n\nSo all tests can use real LooseVersion/StrictVersion instances with explicit `Version.method(instance, ...)` calls where the subclass overrides. For comparisons, LooseVersion doesn't override `__eq__`/`__lt__`/etc., so normal operators hit `Version`'s methods directly. \n\nNow the comparison tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_eq_zero_and_not_implemented():\n    a, b = LooseVersion('1.2.3'), LooseVersion('1.2.3')\n    # c == 0 branch (lines 54, 57)\n    assert a == b\n    assert Version.__eq__(a, b) is True\n    assert not (a != b)\n    # NotImplemented branch (lines 55-56)\n    assert Version.__eq__(a, object()) is NotImplemented\n```\n\nWait: `a == b` \u2014 does `LooseVersion.__eq__` exist? get_info showed LooseVersion's methods: `__init__`, `parse`, `__str__`, `__repr__`, `_cmp`. So `__eq__` inherited from Version. Good. `a == b` \u2192 `Version.__eq__(a, b)` \u2192 `a._cmp(b)` \u2192 both LooseVersion, versions equal \u2192 0 \u2192 `c == 0` \u2192 True. Hits lines 54, 57.\n\n`Version.__eq__(a, 42)` \u2192 `a._cmp(42)` \u2192 not str, not LooseVersion \u2192 NotImplemented \u2192 `c is NotImplemented` \u2192 return c (lines 55-56).\n\nAlso StrictVersion: `StrictVersion('1.0') == '1.0'` \u2192 `_cmp('1.0')` converts string \u2192 0 \u2192 True. And `StrictVersion('1.0').__eq__(42)` \u2192 NotImplemented.\n\nTest `__lt__` (lines 60-63):\n\n```python\ndef test_lt():\n    assert LooseVersion('1.2') < LooseVersion('1.3')      # c=-1 -> True (lines 60, 63)\n    assert LooseVersion('1.3') < LooseVersion('1.2') is False  # careful with chaining!\n```\n\nCareful: `LooseVersion('1.3') < LooseVersion('1.2') is False` \u2014 operator chaining! `a < b is False` parses as `(a < b) and (b is False)` \u2014 bug. Must use parentheses: `assert (LooseVersion('1.3') < LooseVersion('1.2')) is False`. Or just `assert not (a < b)`.\n\nNotImplemented branch: `(LooseVersion('1.2')).__lt__(42) is NotImplemented`. And operator form raises TypeError:\n\n```python\nwith pytest.raises(TypeError):\n    LooseVersion('1.2') < 42\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `LooseVersion('1.2') < 42`: left `__lt__` returns NotImplemented \u2192 Python tries `42.__gt__(lv)` \u2192 int's `__gt__` returns NotImplemented \u2192 TypeError. Yes.\n\nSimilarly for `<=`, `>`, `>=`. Note `LooseVersion('1.2') <= LooseVersion('1.2')` \u2192 c=0 \u2192 `0 <= 0` True. `LooseVersion('1.3') <= LooseVersion('1.2')` \u2192 c=1 \u2192 False. `LooseVersion('1.2') >= LooseVersion('1.2')` \u2192 True. `LooseVersion('1.1') >= LooseVersion('1.2')` \u2192 False. `LooseVersion('1.3') > LooseVersion('1.2')` \u2192 True; `LooseVersion('1.2') > LooseVersion('1.3')` \u2192 False.\n\nAlso StrictVersion prerelease comparisons exercise various `_cmp` return values through Version rich comparisons:\n- `StrictVersion('1.0a1') == StrictVersion('1.0a1')` \u2192 True\n- `StrictVersion('1.0a1') < StrictVersion('1.0')` \u2192 True (prerelease less)\n- `StrictVersion('1.0') > StrictVersion('1.0b2')` \u2192 True\n- `StrictVersion('1.0a1') <= StrictVersion('1.0a1')` \u2192 True\n- `StrictVersion('1.0a1') < StrictVersion('1.0a2')` \u2192 True\n\nThese hit lines 54-57/60-63/66-69/72-75/78-81 too via real subclasses.\n\nFor NotImplemented on the operator level with StrictVersion: `StrictVersion('1.0') < object()`? Wait, `Version.__lt__` returns NotImplemented \u2192 reflected: `object().__lt__`... the reflected op for `<` is `__gt__` of the other operand. `object` has default `__gt__` which returns NotImplemented \u2192 TypeError. So `pytest.raises(TypeError): StrictVersion('1.0') < object()`. Hmm, actually default object comparisons: `object() < object()` raises TypeError in Py3. Yes.\n\nBut careful: equality \u2014 `LooseVersion('1.2') == 42`: `__eq__` returns NotImplemented \u2192 falls back to `42.__eq__(lv)` \u2192 NotImplemented \u2192 then identity comparison \u2192 False. So `assert LooseVersion('1.2') == 42` is False \u2014 no exception. Good, but the return value is identity-based False, not our NotImplemented. To verify our method returns NotImplemented, call the dunder directly.\n\nAlso test `__ne__`? Not in missing lines; skip. Note: Python auto-derives `__ne__` from `__eq__` (unless overridden). Not needed.\n\nNow cleanup/state pollution: no global state modified. The tests create only local objects. No monkeypatch needed. Maybe I should note that nothing is persisted. There's no fixture cleanup required. I could add a small autouse fixture? Not necessary. I'll keep it clean without.\n\nOne more consideration: the module might use `from ansible.module_utils.compat.version import ...`. Some compat modules do sys.modules patching (e.g., replacing distutils.version). The import of `ansible.module_utils.compat.version` itself should be fine.\n\nLet me also double check `LooseVersion.parse` behavior for '1.2.3': `component_re.split('1.2.3')` \u2192 pattern `(\\d+ | [a-z]+ | \\.)` with re.VERBOSE. split gives `['', '1', '.', '2', '.', '3', '']` \u2192 filtered \u2192 `['1', '2', '3']` \u2192 ints \u2192 `[1, 2, 3]`. Good.\n\n`LooseVersion('1.2') < LooseVersion('1.3')` \u2192 `[1,2] < [1,3]` \u2192 True \u2192 -1. Good.\n\nMixed int/str components could raise TypeError in Py3 for comparisons like `[1, 'a'] < [1, 'b']`? No, that compares 'a' vs 'b' fine. `[1, 'a'] < [1, 2]` would raise TypeError. Not needed.\n\nNow write the NotImplemented direct-call assertions for all five comparison methods to hit branches 55-56, 61-62, 67-68, 73-74, 79-80:\n\n```python\ndef test_comparisons_return_not_implemented_for_unsupported_types():\n    lv = LooseVersion('1.2')\n    assert Version.__eq__(lv, object()) is NotImplemented\n    assert Version.__lt__(lv, object()) is NotImplemented\n    assert Version.__le__(lv, object()) is NotImplemented\n    assert Version.__gt__(lv, object()) is NotImplemented\n    assert Version.__ge__(lv, object()) is NotImplemented\n```\n\nWait \u2014 does calling `Version.__lt__(lv, obj)` hit lines 60-63? Yes: `c = self._cmp(other)` (line 60), `_cmp` returns NotImplemented, `if c is NotImplemented: return c` (61-62). Good. And for the truthy comparisons (line 63 etc.), use operators or direct calls:\n\n```python\ndef test_comparisons_operators():\n    a = LooseVersion('1.2')\n    b = LooseVersion('1.3')\n    assert a == LooseVersion('1.2')\n    assert (a < b) is True\n    assert (b < a) is False\n    assert (a <= a) is True\n    assert (b <= a) is False\n    assert (b > a) is True\n    assert (a > b) is False\n    assert (a >= a) is True\n    assert (a >= b) is False\n```\n\n`assert (a < b) is True` \u2014 `Version.__lt__` returns `c < 0` which is a bool. Good.\n\nEach of these hits line 63 (a<b: c=-1 <0 True; b<a: c=1<0 False \u2014 both hit line 63 since it's the `return c < 0` line; branches within the expression aren't tracked